{"body": "## Case A\n\nThe reporting specification is explicit: subtract the sum of the selected quantities from the capacity and return the resulting integer.\n\nSelected quantities: 6 (booking K) and 5 (booking L). Sum = 11. Capacity = 9.\n\nReporting result: 9 − 11 = −2.\n\nWhat follows about the selected entries: very little beyond the arithmetic. The specification permits a negative return; it does not say a negative result invalidates the file, rejects either entry, or indicates that any selection was wrong. It also does not say the two entries are jointly performable, jointly non-performable, or that the holds overlap in any physical sense. The case describes them as “active holds” in a reporting file with no ties, releases, or expiries, and each booking has exactly that one selected entry, so there is no within-booking supersession puzzle and no tie to preserve.\n\nA further premise would be needed for any stronger conclusion. For example:\n\n- If one added “holds whose total exceeds capacity are inadmissible,” then −2 would signal an over-reservation, not merely a negative number. But that is an exclusion rule, not part of the supplied specification.\n- If one added “the two holds concern the same single-use resource at the same time,” then joint performance would be blocked. The case supplies no such resource field.\n- If one added “the reporting result is intended as a planner-facing availability figure rather than a subtraction output,” then −2 might have downstream significance. But the case names only the reporting file and its calculation.\n\nSo Case A supports the subtraction result −2 and essentially nothing about whether the selected entries can coexist physically, legally, or operationally. The stronger conclusion requires a premise the case does not supply.\n\n## Case B under the specified reporting procedure\n\nCapacity = 23. Booking U alternatives: 0 or 8. Booking V alternatives: 0 or 11. The procedure forms every pair (u,v) and displays 23 − u − v.\n\nPair-level work:\n\n| u | v | 23 − u − v |\n|---|---|---|\n| 0 | 0 | 23 |\n| 0 | 11 | 12 |\n| 8 | 0 | 15 |\n| 8 | 11 | 4 |\n\nSo the specified reporting procedure displays: 23, 12, 15, 4.\n\nThese are exactly the values the procedure calls for; the procedure as stated does not exclude any pair.\n\n## Case B with the inspection file available\n\nThe inspection file stipulates that U = 8 and V = 11 both need the same single-use jig at the same time, so the pair (8, 11) cannot be performed jointly. Each of the other listed pairs can be performed jointly. The planner asks for 23 − u − v for pairs that can be performed jointly.\n\nCheckable pair-level work under the inspection:\n\n| u | v | 23 − u − v | jointly performable? | planner result |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 0 | 0 | 23 | yes | 23 |\n| 0 | 11 | 12 | yes | 12 |\n| 8 | 0 | 15 | yes | 15 |\n| 8 | 11 | 4 | no | — |\n\nSo the planner’s requested results are: 23, 12, 15. The value 4 is computed by the unrestricted reporting procedure but is not returned to the planner, because the inspection excludes that pair.\n\nThis is a place where the distinction between the reporting procedure and the inspection file matters. The reporting procedure is well-defined over all pairs and yields four values. The inspection narrows the admissible pairs to three and therefore drops one value. Neither the snapshot nor the procedure alone performs that narrowing.\n\n## A reader with only the snapshot and reporting procedure\n\nSuch a reader can support:\n\n- The four pair values 23, 12, 15, and 4, as outputs of the stated calculation.\n- The observation that these values range from 4 to 23 and reflect the two bookings’ listed alternatives.\n- The observation that the snapshot contains no physical-resource fields, so it does not itself supply evidence about shared jigs, simultaneous use, or any other cross-booking physical constraint.\n\nSuch a reader cannot support:\n\n- That all four pairs are jointly performable.\n- That any particular pair is impossible.\n- That the pair (8, 11) is or is not blocked by an unnamed resource.\n- That the value 4 is or is not available to a planner.\n- That the snapshot is complete with respect to physical resources rather than merely silent about them.\n\nThe reader can say that a pairing “might” fail for an unnamed reason, but cannot identify which pairing or what reason. The snapshot’s silence is compatible both with “no such resource matters” and with “such a resource exists but is not named.” The inspection file is what breaks that symmetry for this finite case.\n\n## The reviewer’s remark, considered before the inspection\n\nThe remark says: “Each listed quantity may be defensible on its own, yet a pairing might fail to be jointly performable. A resource that the snapshot does not name might matter.”\n\nAt the time it was made, the remark supplies no observed resource and no identified impossible pair. It is a conditional warning, not an observation.\n\nWhat commitments does it challenge? It challenges any commitment that treats per-booking defensibility as sufficient for joint performability. More specifically, it challenges:\n\n1. A commitment that the cross-booking alternative set can be projected by independent per-booking reasoning without a further composition premise.\n2. A commitment that the snapshot’s list of fields is complete for the purpose of determining joint performability.\n3. A commitment that the reporting calculation’s four values are all planner-usable simply because they are arithmetically correct.\n\nIt does not challenge the listed quantities 0, 8, 0, 11 themselves. A quantity can be defensible for its own booking and still participate in a joint failure. It also does not challenge the arithmetic 23 − u − v. The calculation remains what it is; what is challenged is the inference from calculated value to realizable availability.\n\nWhere would the challenge affect the use of an account? At the point where the account moves from reporting to planning, or from per-booking representation to cross-booking composition. If an account only claims “here is what the bag supports,” the remark does not require a change. If an account claims “here is what a planner can rely on,” the remark identifies a defeasibility condition: the composition assumption is not guaranteed by the snapshot.\n\nDefensible alternative readings of the remark:\n\n- Strong reading: the snapshot is inadequate for any planner-facing use until physical-resource information is added. On this reading, the remark is a demand for more fields or a resource model.\n- Weak reading: the snapshot is adequate for reporting but defeasible for planning; the remark marks a conditional limitation, not a defect.\n- Skeptical reading: the remark is idle speculation because it supplies no resource and no impossible pair; without an observation, no change is warranted.\n\nA case or observation that would distinguish them: the inspection file itself. It supplies an actual resource (the single-use jig) and an actual impossible pair ((8, 11)). That converts the weak reading into a demonstrated limitation for this case and supports the strong reading for planner-facing use in this case. It does not by itself show that the strong reading is correct for all snapshots or that any unnamed resource always matters. A different observation—say, a case where all pairs are performable and no shared resource exists—would support the skeptical reading for that case.\n\nDoes the remark alone require changing a listed quantity or the reporting calculation? No. The grounds: the remark asserts a possibility (“might fail,” “might matter”), not a fact. It names no resource, no impossible pair, and no alternative value. Changing a listed quantity or the arithmetic would require a premise the remark does not supply. The remark can warrant marking an assumption as defeasible, or asking for an inspection, or qualifying a planner-facing claim. It does not by itself warrant rewriting 8, 11, or 23 − u − v.\n\n## Relating the treatment of the reviewer to the numerical answers\n\nThere is a tension worth naming. The reporting procedure yields four values: 23, 12, 15, 4. The planner’s request, with inspection, yields three: 23, 12, 15. The reviewer’s remark, before inspection, does not choose between these outcomes. It says a pairing might fail; it does not say which one does. So the remark is compatible with the unreduced four-value output and with the reduced three-value output. It does not by itself license dropping 4, and it does not by itself license keeping 4 for planner use.\n\nThe distinction is between arithmetic output and planner-admissible output. The reporting procedure is an arithmetic operation over all listed pairs. The planner’s request is a filtered operation over performable pairs. The inspection file supplies the filter. The reviewer’s remark, without the inspection, supplies only the possibility of a filter, not the filter itself.\n\nAn unresolved issue remains: whether an account that reports only the arithmetic outputs is required to change in the presence of the conditional warning, or whether marking the composition assumption as defeasible is sufficient. The supplied material does not settle that. Case B with inspection settles it for that finite case by supplying an actual blocked pair, but it does not settle the general question. Nor does the remark alone settle it.\n\n## On the supplied account\n\nThe account is relevant here mainly for its distinction between “capacity as a shared input to reporting” and “capacity as an exclusion rule.” That distinction bears directly on Case A. Under the reporting reading, −2 is a permitted output and does not exclude either hold. Under the exclusion reading, a total above capacity would be inadmissible; but Case A’s specification does not state that reading, and its negative-output possibility suggests the reporting reading. I use the distinction but do not treat it as supplied by the case material; the account adds it, and I record that addition as an addition.\n\nThe account’s three loci—within-booking, cross-booking complementation, and coordinate—also bear on Case B. The reviewer’s remark targets the cross-booking complementation locus, not the listed quantities. The inspection file supplies a cross-booking incompatibility at that locus. The account’s caution that the incompatibility is conditional rather than observed matches the reviewer’s status before inspection, and the inspection file is exactly the observation that converts the conditional into a demonstrated limitation for this case.\n\nI depart from the account in one respect: it leaves unresolved whether an account reporting only what the bag supports must change. For Case B, that is the right stance when only the snapshot and reviewer’s remark are in view. But once the inspection file is available, the planner’s request has a determinate answer, and no further choice between “report what the bag supports” and “report what a planner could realize” is needed for this finite case. The general unresolved question remains, but it should not be allowed to obscure the case-specific result: with inspection, the planner gets 23, 12, and 15, not 4.\n\nThe account’s passages on negative availability and on the conditional cross-booking objection are the ones I use most directly. I qualify its “independence given capacity” discussion only to say that Case A’s specification is best read as the reporting reading, not the exclusion reading, because it returns an integer without stating an admissibility rule. That is a reading of the supplied case, not a change to the account’s general framework.", "commitments": "## Case A\n\nThe reporting specification is explicit: subtract the sum of the selected quantities from the capacity and return the resulting integer.\n\nSelected quantities: 6 (booking K) and 5 (booking L). Sum = 11. Capacity = 9.\n\nReporting result: 9 − 11 = −2.\n\nWhat fo