k, before inspection, does not choose between these outcomes. It says a pairing might fail; it does not say which one does. So the remark is compatible with the unreduced four-value output and with the reduced three-value output. It does not by itself license dropping 4, and it does not by itself license keeping 4 for planner use.\n\nThe distinction is between arithmetic output and planner-admissible output. The reporting procedure is an arithmetic operation over all listed pairs. The planner’s request is a filtered operation over performable pairs. The inspection file supplies the filter. The reviewer’s remark, without the inspection, supplies only the possibility of a filter, not the filter itself.\n\nAn unresolved issue remains: whether an account that reports only the arithmetic outputs is required to change in the presence of the conditional warning, or whether marking the composition assumption as defeasible is sufficient. The supplied material does not settle that. Case B with inspection settles it for that finite case by supplying an actual blocked pair, but it does not settle the general question. Nor does the remark alone settle it.\n\n## On the supplied account\n\nThe account is relevant here mainly for its distinction between “capacity as a shared input to reporting” and “capacity as an exclusion rule.” That distinction bears directly on Case A. Under the reporting reading, −2 is a permitted output and does not exclude either hold. Under the exclusion reading, a total above capacity would be inadmissible; but Case A’s specification does not state that reading, and its negative-output possibility suggests the reporting reading. I use the distinction but do not treat it as supplied by the case material; the account adds it, and I record that addition as an addition.\n\nThe account’s three loci—within-booking, cross-booking complementation, and coordinate—also bear on Case B. The reviewer’s remark targets the cross-booking complementation locus, not the listed quantities. The inspection file supplies a cross-booking incompatibility at that locus. The account’s caution that the incompatibility is conditional rather than observed matches the reviewer’s status before inspection, and the inspection file is exactly the observation that converts the conditional into a demonstrated limitation for this case.\n\nI depart from the account in one respect: it leaves unresolved whether an account reporting only what the bag supports must change. For Case B, that is the right stance when only the snapshot and reviewer’s remark are in view. But once the inspection file is available, the planner’s request has a determinate answer, and no further choice between “report what the bag supports” and “report what a planner could realize” is needed for this finite case. The general unresolved question remains, but it should not be allowed to obscure the case-specific result: with inspection, the planner gets 23, 12, and 15, not 4.\n\nThe account’s passages on negative availability and on the conditional cross-booking objection are the ones I use most directly. I qualify its “independence given capacity” discussion only to say that Case A’s specification is best read as the reporting reading, not the exclusion reading, because it returns an integer without stating an admissibility rule. That is a reading of the supplied case, not a change to the account’s general framework."}